\title{solomonR: Analyze Solomon Four-Group Designs}
\description{
Provides tools for analyzing, teaching, and studying the Solomon
four-group experimental design. The package includes the historical
Tests A-I workflow, unified generalized linear models with robust
covariance estimation, stratified randomization inference,
Solomon-specific full-information maximum likelihood, and observed-
and latent-variable structural equation models.
}
\details{
The package distinguishes historically important procedures from
contemporary recommendations and emphasizes explicit Solomon-specific
estimands, structural pretest missingness, reproducible diagnostics,
and modern inference.

Key historical references include Huck & Sandler (1973),
Walton Braver & Braver (1988), the Sawilowsky and Markman methodological
exchanges, and van Engelenburg (1999).
}
\section{Lifecycle}{

Each exported function's help page, and the reference index of the
package website, shows the function's lifecycle stage, in the stages of
the lifecycle package (Henry & Wickham, 2026):
\itemize{
\item \strong{Stable.} The interface is settled. Any change goes through
deprecation: the old name keeps working, with a warning, through v1.x,
when the arguments that follow a former argument name are also named.
\item \strong{Experimental.} The function is tested, but its interface or defaults
may change. \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem]{fit_solomon_sem()}} has no simulation study yet at Solomon
sample sizes. The issue #55 study found no measurement-invariance
criterion that holds its false-rejection rate in Solomon-sized groups,
which affects \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}} and \code{\link[=invariance_solomon]{invariance_solomon()}}.
The analysis of designs with several treatments in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} is
experimental, because the rule for error control set before its
simulation study (issue #45) was not met for two omnibus tests with
three treatments and 10 participants per group. The pre-specified
rules of their simulation studies were not met for \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}}
(issue #57) and for \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mi]{fit_solomon_mi()}}, \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}}, and
\code{\link[=plot_tipping_point]{plot_tipping_point()}} (issue #82). \code{\link[=analysis_plan_solomon]{analysis_plan_solomon()}} stays
experimental until researchers have used it (issue #81).
\item \strong{Deprecated.} A former name that still works, with a warning. Its
help page names the replacement.
}

solomonR 0.8.0 settled the interface (issue #83). The outcome arguments
are \code{y_post} and \code{y_pre} everywhere, a fitted model is passed as \code{fit},
and the planning functions share one argument order: \code{n} (or \code{power}),
\code{delta}, \code{sens}, \code{rho}, \code{sigma}, \code{alpha}. Each function that takes data
vectors also takes an optional \code{data} data frame.

solomonR 1.0.0 settled the output (issue #110). Every analysis that
estimates the Solomon contrasts returns them in a table named \code{effects},
with one order of columns and one set of contrast labels, beside the
confidence level \code{conf_level}, and \code{\link[=tidy]{tidy()}} returns that table.
\link{solomon_output} states which parts of a result are stable and which are
not.
}

